     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>(1) Assuming I&#39;ve verified there are no significant genotype X year interactions - Would it be correct to combine the phenotype results into one \r\nlarge cross file with the year as covariate (same genotype would appear 3\r\n times next to its result)?</div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>This won&#39;t work properly. You need to either take averages across the years or use a mixed model to account for the within-line correlations, though the latter is not something you can do in R/qtl. </div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div></div><div>(2) I&#39;ve noticed in the summary of fitqtl that the LOD score of the first qtl step in `stepwiseqtl` (8...@67.9 - LOD 33.7) is much larger than when building the model manually with scanone and addqtl. Is this correct?  <br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> The first step of stepwiseqtl is to run either scanone() or addqtl(), and so the results of the first step should be identical.</div><div><br></div><div>karl</div></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
